<ModuleLayout moduleId="eyes" activeTab="overview" label="Clinical Module" title="👁️ Eyes — Ophthalmology" description="Assessment framework, SOAP note, resources, and quiz for ophthalmic presentations" activePage="eyes">
<div class="content-prose">
<h2>Clinical Overview</h2>
<p>Ophthalmic presentations are common in primary care. As a Nurse Practitioner, you must distinguish <strong>benign self-limiting conditions</strong> (e.g. viral conjunctivitis) from <strong>urgent sight-threatening emergencies</strong> (e.g. acute angle-closure glaucoma) that require immediate ophthalmology referral.</p>
<div class="highlight-box"><h4>⚠️ Red Flags — Refer Urgently</h4><ul><li>Sudden loss of vision (partial or complete)</li><li>Painful red eye with decreased visual acuity</li><li>Fixed, dilated or irregularly shaped pupil</li><li>Halos around lights with headache and nausea</li><li>Eye trauma, chemical injury, or foreign body</li><li>Proptosis or orbital swelling</li><li>Diplopia of new onset</li></ul></div>
<h2>Common Presentations</h2>
<div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:16px">
<div class="assessment-card"><div class="card-header"><div class="icon-circle">🔴</div><h4>Red Eye Differentials</h4></div><ul><li>Viral conjunctivitis (most common)</li><li>Bacterial conjunctivitis</li><li>Allergic conjunctivitis</li><li>Subconjunctival haemorrhage</li><li>Episcleritis / Scleritis</li><li>Acute anterior uveitis</li><li>Acute angle-closure glaucoma ⚠️</li><li>Keratitis</li></ul></div>
<div class="assessment-card"><div class="card-header"><div class="icon-circle">👁️</div><h4>Vision Change Causes</h4></div><ul><li>Refractive error (common, benign)</li><li>Cataracts (gradual, painless)</li><li>Age-related macular degeneration</li><li>Diabetic retinopathy</li><li>Central retinal artery/vein occlusion ⚠️</li><li>Retinal detachment ⚠️</li><li>Optic neuritis (consider MS)</li><li>Glaucoma (insidious field loss)</li></ul></div>
</div>
</div>
